From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:36:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10555/11362] Record that the 9-game adapter-free run is
 uninterpretable

A 22-hour run of arc_leaderboard_eval --policy e3 over the CLAIMED set produced
per-game numbers that cannot be read as an e3 result: nothing in the artifact
records whether each game actually had a generator.

One llama-server fit at first launch and then held ~18.4 GB for the whole run, so
every later game logged "even 12 CPU-FFN layers cannot fit the generator" and
failed to load its own. Of 23 server logs from the run, exactly ONE has any
print_timing lines (32,833); the other 22 have zero. Whether later games reached
the surviving server over HTTP was confirmed exactly once, by direct socket
inspection.

The per-game rows carry no llm, induction, generator or proposer field, so
"levels=2, eff=0.1025" is indistinguishable between the e3 cascade solving it and
the explorer floor solving it with no model at all. tu93 wrote no per-run file;
sp80/su15 wrote two conflicting ones.

Two of my own earlier conclusions are corrected in the entry rather than dropped.
I read ppid 1 as abandonment and nearly killed the server mid-run; then, having
confirmed a live socket, I concluded the reuse was harmless. It was both
load-bearing and harmful at different times, and each single observation
supported only half of that.

Defensible result: two games. cd82 (2 levels, 0.0267) and r11l (2 levels, 0.6890)
had a verified generator; r11l is the outlier that made the whole set look
encouraging. The entry says explicitly not to cite "7 levels across 9 games", and
notes the dashboard's generaliz. line is currently reporting exactly that.

The fix is small and is the task: record per-game generator provenance -- resolved
backend, whether a load succeeded, completions consumed. Without it no multi-game
run of this script is interpretable, and these 22 hours bought two games.

